- A. Sharp and well localised
- B. Vague and midline
- C. Absent, as it is insensitive
- D. Felt only on stretching
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The greater and lesser sacs communicate through the epiploic foramen, also called the foramen of Winslow or the opening of the lesser sac." %}
The greater sac and the lesser sac communicate through the:
- A. Falciform ligament
- B. Hepatoduodenal ligament
- C. Epiploic foramen
- D. Paracolic gutter
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The greater omentum seals off the site of a ruptured appendix or gastric ulcer and delays peritonitis, which is why it is called the abdominal policeman." %}
The greater omentum is called the "abdominal policeman" because it:
- A. Supports the small intestine
- B. Stores fat
- C. Connects the liver to the diaphragm
- D. Limits the spread of infection
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The duodenum, pancreas, kidney, ureter and suprarenal gland are retroperitoneal. The stomach, jejunum, ileum and spleen are intraperitoneal." %}
Which of the following is a retroperitoneal organ?
- A. Jejunum
- B. Pancreas
- C. Stomach
- D. Spleen
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In the female the peritoneum passes from the rectum to the uterus (rectouterine pouch) and from the uterus to the bladder (vesicouterine pouch). In the male it passes directly from the rectum to the bladder, forming the rectovesical pouch." %}
In the female the peritoneum passes from the front of the rectum to the uterus, forming the:
- A. Rectovesical pouch
- B. Vesicouterine pouch
- C. Rectouterine pouch
- D. Epiploic foramen
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The visceral peritoneum is supplied by splanchnic vessels, and its venous blood returns by the portal vein. The parietal peritoneum drains to the inferior vena cava." %}
The venous blood of the visceral peritoneum drains into the:
- A. Portal vein
- B. Inferior vena cava
- C. Internal jugular vein
- D. Azygos vein
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the peritoneum, and what are its layers?" %}
The largest serous membrane of the body, lining the abdominal cavity as a closed sac. The parietal layer lines the walls and the visceral layer covers the organs. A mesothelial layer lines the surface, with fibrous tissue beneath it.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why do parietal and visceral peritoneum produce different kinds of pain?" %}
Parietal peritoneum has somatic nerves and gives sharp, localised pain. Visceral peritoneum has autonomic afferents, responds only to stretch, and gives vague midline pain.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the parts of the peritoneal cavity and how they connect." %}
The greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa). They connect through the epiploic foramen (foramen of Winslow).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the boundaries of the epiploic foramen?" %}
Posteriorly the inferior vena cava, right suprarenal gland and T12 vertebra. Superiorly the caudate process of the liver. Inferiorly the first part of the duodenum and the horizontal part of the hepatic artery.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the functions of the greater omentum?" %}
It limits the spread of infection by sealing off a ruptured appendix or ulcer, stores fat, and allows free movement of the viscera.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is ascites and how is it treated by the abdominal wall route?" %}
Free fluid in the peritoneal cavity, commonly from cirrhosis, tubercular peritonitis, heart failure or malignancy. Paracentesis removes it through the midline between the umbilicus and pubic symphysis, or just above the anterior superior iliac spine, after emptying the bladder.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which route does infected fluid take to the subphrenic space?" %}
Fluid ascends from the lower abdomen and pelvis through the right paracolic gutter, which is deeper than the left, to the right subhepatic and subphrenic spaces.
{% /viva %}

